sudo usermod [options] username
usermod (User Modify) is a Linux command used to modify an existing user account. It allows you to change a user's login name,
home directory, user ID (UID), primary group, secondary groups, login shell, and other account settings.
Why do we use usermod?
Change a username.
Add a user to a group.
Change the home directory.
Change the login shell.
Change the User ID (UID).
Lock or unlock a user account.

Option	Description
-aG	Add user to a supplementary group
-g	Change primary group
-l	Change username (login name)
-d	Change home directory
-m	Move contents to the new home directory (used with -d)
-s	Change login shell
-u	Change User ID (UID)
-L	Lock the user account
-U	Unlock the user account

sudo usermod -aG docker siva
sudo usermod -l sivakumar siva  Changes username from siva to sivakumar.
sudo usermod -d /home/sivakumar -m sivakumar
-d = New home directory
-m = Move existing files to the new home directory
sudo usermod -s /bin/bash siva  Sets the default shell to Bash.
sudo usermod -u 1050 siva  Changes the user's UID to 1050.
sudo usermod -L siva       The user cannot log in until the account is unlocked.
sudo usermod -U siva       Unlock a user account
